---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'tax', label: 'Debugging by redeploy' },
  { id: 'launch', label: 'Launch the worker under your debugger' },
  { id: 'pause', label: 'Pause inside the handler' },
  { id: 'state', label: 'Check what the cloud stored' },
];
const startCmd = `localcloud start --local-only
localcloud env`;
const vscodeEnv = `{
  "name": "Debug Pub/Sub worker (LocalCloud)",
  "type": "debugpy",
  "request": "launch",
  "program": "\${workspaceFolder}/worker.py",
  "env": {
    "GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT": "local-gcp-project",
    "PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST": "127.0.0.1:5383",
    "STORAGE_EMULATOR_HOST": "http://127.0.0.1:5382"
  }
}`;
const inspectCmd = `# Open the data browser and request log
localcloud console

# Or follow the runtime's logs in a terminal
localcloud logs --tail 50`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="step-through-breakpoints-gcp"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="Add a log line, build, deploy, trigger the event, wait for the log to show up, realise you logged the wrong variable. There is a faster way to see what a cloud handler is doing: stop it on a breakpoint."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/console/', label: 'Web console guide' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/docs/', label: 'Quick start' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="Your worker running under the IDE debugger against local Pub/Sub and Cloud Storage, with a breakpoint inside the message handler."
  needs="VS Code with the Python debugger, or the equivalent run configuration in your IDE"
  next={{ posts: ['reproduce-gcp-incidents-locally', 'the-2ms-inner-loop'], doc: { href: '/docs/console/', label: 'Web console: data browser, SQL explorers and request logs' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="tax">Debugging by redeploy</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">When a consumer drops an attribute or an insert rejects a nested field in a cloud dev environment, the debugger can't reach it. So you fall back to print statements, and each one costs a build, a deploy and a wait for the log pipeline.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The question was small: what is in this variable at this line? The answer takes most of an hour because the code is running somewhere you can't pause it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="launch">Launch the worker under your debugger</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud serves the same gRPC and HTTP APIs on localhost, so the worker can run as an ordinary local process. Start the runtime and print its endpoints:</p>
    <BlogCode label="Start and list endpoints" code={startCmd} copy="copy-blog-breakpoints-start" />
    <p class="mt-4">Copy the values into a launch configuration. The ones below are the defaults; use what <code>localcloud env</code> printed if yours differ.</p>
    <BlogCode label=".vscode/launch.json (one configuration)" code={vscodeEnv} copy="copy-blog-breakpoints-vscode" />
    <p class="mt-4">PyCharm, GoLand and the Node.js debugger take the same three variables in their run configurations. Nothing in the worker changes: the Google Cloud clients read those variables themselves.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="pause">Pause inside the handler</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Set a breakpoint on the first line of your message callback and start the configuration. Publish a message from a test, a script or the console's data browser, and execution stops with the real message object in scope.</p>
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Read the payload as the client delivers it.</strong> Data is bytes and attributes are strings, which is where a lot of handler bugs start.</li>
      <li><strong>Step into the retry path.</strong> Raise before the acknowledgement and watch the message come back.</li>
      <li><strong>Take your time.</strong> Nothing is billing you while you think.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="state">Check what the cloud stored</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">A breakpoint shows you the code's view. The <a href="/docs/console/">web console</a> shows you the other side while the debugger is paused:</p>
    <BlogCode label="Open the console or follow logs" code={inspectCmd} copy="copy-blog-breakpoints-cmd" />
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Data browser.</strong> Look at the objects in a bucket, the messages on a topic and the records your handler wrote.</li>
      <li><strong>SQL explorers.</strong> Query BigQuery or Spanner to confirm the row that was just inserted.</li>
      <li><strong>Request log.</strong> See each API call arrive as you step over the line that makes it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">Between the two you can answer the small question in a minute, and you are still in your editor when you do.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
